# tally: a keyed grid that prints its own weight
#
#     $ vonal run examples/tally.vsr
#     613
#
# Every cell is its own figure and ground pair, in the idiom of Vasarely's
# Plastic Alphabet and the Planetary Folklore series. All eight colours appear
# as grounds and all eight as figures, which the absolute encoding could not
# do: there the variant was the figure colour, so every push was a black disc
# and two of the eight colours were ground-only.
#
# The ground is keyed by (x + y) mod 8, plain diagonals. The figures follow
# from it, since the variant is an offset, so they run diagonally too. Where
# they break the diagonal is exactly where an instruction sits: with an offset
# encoding you choose which channel carries the clean pattern, and the other
# one records where the code is. Ground is the larger area, so it takes the
# pattern and the code shows up as flecks in the figures rather than as damage.
#
# 613 is the sum of all 384 scales. i walks 0 to 383 and reads the cell at
# (i mod 24, i div 24), so every cell on the plate is counted, the loop's own
# cells included. Resize any glyph and the number moves with it.
#
# Column 0 seeds sum and i, then drops the eye into row 3. The loop is entered
# at column 1, which leaves the turn-east at column 0 to double as the wrap
# point, and the stack is [sum, i] at both ends of the row. Leaving it, the eye
# climbs column 22 to drop the finished counter and prints along row 1.
%plate 24x16

v1..  o1.1  o1.2  o1.3  o1.4  o1.5  o1.6  o1.7  o1..  o1.1  o1.2  o1.3  o1.4  o1.5  o1.6  o1.7  o1..  o1.1  o1.2  o1.3  o1.4  o1.5  o1.6  o1.7
o1.1  o1.2  o1.3  o1.4  o1.5  o1.6  o1.7  o1..  o111  o112  o113  o114  o115  o116  o117  o11.  o1.1  ...2  +2.3  o224  o115  +1.6  <1.7  o1..
o1.2  o1.3  o1.4  o1.5  o1.6  o117  o11.  o111  o112  o123  o124  o125  o126  o127  o12.  o111  o112  o113  o114  o1.5  o1.6  o1.7  %2..  o1.1
